# PART 12 — UI/UX: What I'd actually change visually

Now let's discuss the design itself.

I don't think you need a redesign from scratch.

I think you need a refinement of the existing design system.

## 1. Too much empty space

This is one of the most noticeable issues.

Several screenshots have enormous unused areas below their tables and cards.

For example:
- Tasks & Follow-ups.
- Domains & Hosting.
- Vault & Documents.
- Meta Ads.

The problem isn't necessarily that the pages need more content.

The problem is that the content isn't using the available screen space effectively.

My recommendations:

- Introduce useful secondary panels.
- Use two-column layouts where appropriate.
- Introduce contextual information.
- Make tables more informative.
- Use expandable detail sections.

Don't fill empty space with decorative cards just to make the page look occupied.

## 2. Too many separate cards

Your current interface uses cards for almost everything.

Cards are visually attractive, but when every single element is a card, the interface starts feeling fragmented.

I would use three levels of visual hierarchy:

**Level 1 — Major Sections**

Large, clearly separated containers.

**Level 2 — Functional Components**

Tables, charts, forms and lists.

**Level 3 — Information**

Small labels, statuses, metadata and inline metrics.

Not everything needs a border, shadow and rounded container.

## 3. Improve typography hierarchy

Your typography is already good.

However, I'd establish a more consistent system:

| Element | Recommended treatment |
|---|---|
| Page title | 26–30px, semibold |
| Section title | 16–18px, semibold |
| Card heading | 12–14px, medium |
| Main metric | 26–32px |
| Body text | 13–14px |
| Secondary information | 11–12px |

Use secondary text sparingly. Some of the current labels are quite small.

## 4. Standardise buttons

Your application currently has several button styles:

- Black buttons.
- Blue buttons.
- Green buttons.
- White outlined buttons.

These are fine, but establish strict rules.

| Button type | Usage |
|---|---|
| Primary | Main action |
| Secondary | Supporting action |
| Tertiary | Less important action |
| Destructive | Delete or irreversible action |
| Success | Confirm or complete |

For example, adding a client should consistently use the primary button style across the application.

## 5. Improve tables

Your tables are functional but could be more sophisticated.

Introduce:

- Sticky headers.
- Column sorting.
- Adjustable column widths.
- Column visibility controls.
- Pagination.
- Bulk selection.
- Bulk actions.
- Export CSV.
- Saved filters.

Also, make the entire row clickable where appropriate, rather than requiring users to locate a specific action button.

## 6. Introduce contextual side panels

This is one of my favourite recommendations.

Instead of navigating to a completely different page whenever you click something, use a right-side drawer for quick details.

For example:

Clicking a client could open a panel showing:

- Client name.
- Contact information.
- Current project.
- Pending amount.
- Next follow-up.
- Recent activity.

Then provide an option to open the complete Client 360° profile.

This would make navigation much faster.

